-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/special-assessments/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a condo special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A one-time charge on top of regular dues, charged when the association needs money it does not have. Roofs, elevators, structural repair, an insurance shortfall or litigation are the usual causes. It is billed per unit, and it can be thousands of dollars."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays a special assessment when a condo sells?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on when it was charged and what the contract says. An assessment already voted and charged before closing is normally the seller's, but that is a contract term, not an automatic rule. Get it in writing, and get an estoppel or resale statement (a letter from the association stating exactly what is owed on that unit) from the association confirming what is owed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I tell if a special assessment is coming?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Read the last twelve to twenty-four months of board meeting minutes, the reserve study, and the budget. Minutes are where a coming assessment is discussed long before it is charged. Thin reserves against an aging roof or elevator is the classic setup."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a special assessment stop my loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It can. Lenders review a condo project as well as the borrower, and an unresolved assessment tied to safety, structural or habitability repairs can make the whole building ineligible for a conventional loan. Our non-warrantable condos page explains that review."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I refuse to pay a special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not realistically. A properly charged assessment is a debt on the unit and can become a lien. If you believe it was charged improperly, that is a question for a South Carolina attorney, not something to settle by withholding payment."}}]}</script>
