+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a condo special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A one-time bill from the condo association, on top of the regular dues, for a cost the budget and the reserve fund do not pay. Every owner pays a share, by the unit's percentage in the master deed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much can a special assessment be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At one Pawleys Island condo, the board proposed $88,398 per unit for extensive repairs and new windows and sliding doors. In our example, a 120-unit building that still needs $900,000 for a roof bills $5,850 to $10,350 a unit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an HOA charge a special assessment without a vote in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the building's bylaws allow it. South Carolina's condo law leaves how owners are billed to each building's bylaws."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there a limit on special assessments in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina's condo and HOA laws put no cap on a special assessment. The limits are in the building's master deed, covenants and bylaws."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays a special assessment at closing, the buyer or the seller?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The seller pays what was billed before closing, from the sale price. If it is not paid, the buyer is liable with the seller, up to the amount on the association's statement."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the seller have to disclose a special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In most resales, yes. The state's disclosure form asks whether the association has levied any special assessments, and the seller answers yes, no or No Representation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I refuse to pay a special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. An unpaid assessment is a lien on the unit, and the association can foreclose it in court."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I pay a special assessment over time?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only if the board offers a payment plan. No South Carolina statute gives an owner a right to one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a special assessment stop my loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Fannie Mae will not buy a loan on any unit in a building whose special assessment pays for a critical repair that has not been fixed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a special assessment tax deductible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not on a home you live in. On a condo you rent out, an assessment for maintenance is a deductible rental expense, and one for an improvement is recovered through depreciation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a special assessment and a reserve fund?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The reserve fund is money the association saves ahead for big repairs. A special assessment is a bill for what the reserve fund and the budget cannot pay."}}]}</script>
