g></span><span><strong>Your share</strong><span class="t">deed percentage</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>Buyer liable</strong><span class="t">for unpaid bills</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>Can stop</strong><span class="t">a condo loan</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated July 27, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">South Carolina law puts no cap on a condo special assessment, and a buyer can owe the seller's unpaid share.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A special assessment is a one-time bill from a condo association, on top of the regular dues. It pays for a cost the budget and the reserve fund do not. South Carolina law puts no cap on one. Your share is your unit's percentage in the master deed, the recorded document that created the condo.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a condo sells with an assessment unpaid, the buyer is liable with the seller, up to the amount on the association's statement. We read the building's minutes and its list of assessments before you make an offer.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us the building's name. We read its minutes before you make an offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us check a building</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#what-it-is" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What it is</a></li><li><a href="#how-much" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How much</a></li><li><a href="#building-age" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Building age</a></li><li><a href="#votes-and-limits" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Votes and limits</a></li><li><a href="#how-we-spot-one" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How we spot one</a></li><li><a href="#who-pays-at-sale" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Who pays at sale</a></li><li><a href="#loans" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Loans</a></li><li><a href="#not-paying" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Not paying</a></li><li><a href="#tax-and-insurance" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Tax and insurance</a></li><li><a href="#special-assessment-questions" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Special assessment questions</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-it-is"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What it is">What is a special assessment on a condo?</h2><style>main#main .sa-flow{list-style:none;margin:1.4rem 0 .6rem;padding:0;display:grid;gap:.7rem;max-width:760px}@media (min-width:760px){main#main .sa-flow{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))}}main#main .sa-flow li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1.1rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.8),rgba(255,255,255,.56));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 26px -18px rgba(28,32,40,.35)}main#main .sa-flow li.sa-you{border:2px solid var(--brass);background:#fff}main#main .sa-flow .sa-n{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:1.9rem;height:1.9rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:.95rem;margin-bottom:.45rem}main#main .sa-flow li.sa-you .sa-n{background:var(--brass)}main#main .sa-flow b{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.3;margin-bottom:.25rem}main#main .sa-flow span.t{display:block;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.55}main#main .sa-cap{color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.6;max-width:760px;margin:0 0 1.4rem}main#main .sa-case{max-width:760px;margin:1.2rem 0 1.6rem;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.96),rgba(28,32,40,.98));color:var(--ivory);box-shadow:0 26px 60px -30px rgba(28,32,40,.6)}main#main .sa-case .k{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass-2);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 .5rem}main#main .sa-case .big{font-family:var(--serif);font-size:clamp(2.2rem,8vw,3rem);line-height:1;color:#fff;margin:0 0 .3rem}main#main .sa-case .sub{color:rgba(244,239,232,.85);line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 .9rem}main#main .sa-case .row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.5rem 1.4rem;border-top:1px solid rgba(244,239,232,.2);padding-top:.8rem;color:rgba(244,239,232,.85);line-height:1.5}main#main .sa-case .row strong{color:#fff;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;margin-right:.35rem}main#main .sa-words{max-width:760px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;padding:1rem 1.15rem 1.1rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.78),rgba(255,255,255,.55));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 26px -18px rgba(28,32,40,.35)}main#main .sa-words p{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 .6rem}main#main .sa-words ul{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.45rem}main#main .sa-words li{margin:0;padding:.35rem .75rem;border-radius:999px;background:var(--ivory-2);color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;line-height:1.35;border:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.08)}main#main .sa-calc{background:var(--navy);border-radius:14px;padding:1.3rem 1.3rem 1.2rem;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;max-width:600px}main#main .sa-calc .k{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass-2);margin:0 0 .9rem;font-weight:600}main#main .sa-calc label{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:.8rem;color:rgba(244,239,232,.92);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.35}main#main .sa-calc .u{white-space:nowrap;color:rgba(244,239,232,.6)}main#main .sa-calc input{width:8.4rem;background:rgba(244,239,232,.12);border:1px solid rgba(244,239,232,.4);border-radius:8px;color:#fff;font:inherit;font-size:1rem;padding:.45rem .55rem;text-align:right}main#main .sa-calc .out{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:1rem;border-top:1px solid rgba(244,239,232,.22);margin-top:1.1rem;padding-top:.9rem;color:rgba(244,239,232,.92);font-family:var(--sans)}main#main .sa-calc .out strong{color:var(--brass-2);font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.8rem;line-height:1}main#main .sa-calc .note{color:rgba(244,239,232,.78);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.55;margin:.6rem 0 0}@media (max-width:420px){main#main .sa-calc label{flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start;gap:.3rem}main#main .sa-calc input{width:100%}main#main .sa-calc .u{width:100%}}main#main .detail-hero .detail-sub{display:none}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A special assessment is a bill the association sends every owner, once, for a cost its budget and its reserve fund cannot pay. Dues pay the yearly budget. The <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">reserve fund</a> is money the association saves for big repairs, such as a roof. A special assessment pays the rest.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a South Carolina condo, a big repair is paid in this order. When a part of the building the association must insure is damaged, the association must repair it promptly. The exceptions are a repair the law does not allow, or a vote by 80 percent of owners not to rebuild.</p><ol class="sa-flow" aria-label="Who pays for a big repair in a South Carolina condo, in order"><li><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><b>The building's insurance</b><span class="t">The association must insure the building.</span></li><li><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><b>The reserve fund</b><span class="t">The money the association has saved for big repairs.</span></li><li class="sa-you"><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><b>The owners</b><span class="t">The rest, split by each unit's percentage in the master deed. This is the special assessment.</span></li></ol><p class="sa-cap">Who pays for a big repair in a South Carolina condo, in order. A building's bylaws can split the owners' part another way.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Repair cost above the insurance and the reserve is a common expense shared by all owners. The board can bill that shortfall as a special assessment, or add it to the yearly budget.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina's condo law does not apply to a neighborhood of houses. There, the HOA's recorded covenants say whether it can charge a special assessment. <a href="/hoa/south-carolina-hoa-laws/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">South Carolina's HOA laws</a> has the rules for both.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-much"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How much">How much is a condo special assessment?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It can be a few thousand dollars a unit or tens of thousands. The size depends on the repair, what the reserve fund holds, and your unit's percentage.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A Pawleys Island case</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At Shipyard Village, a condo in Pawleys Island, the board said two of its buildings needed extensive repairs. Every unit's windows and sliding doors had to be replaced, at $12 million to $13 million. A consultant found corroded reinforcing steel and an outer shell that was not weather tight.</p><div class="sa-case"><p class="k">A Pawleys Island condo</p><p class="big">$88,398</p><p class="sub">The special assessment the board proposed for each unit in Buildings A and B at Shipyard Village.</p><div class="row"><span><strong>$64,868</strong>three-bedroom units in Buildings C and D</span><span><strong>$68,471</strong>four-bedroom units in Buildings C and D</span></div></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How your share is worked out</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Example, with our numbers:</strong> a 120-unit building needs a $1.2 million roof and has $300,000 saved for it. The $900,000 still needed is split by each unit's percentage in the master deed.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Example: a $1.2 million roof with $300,000 saved</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Unit</th><th scope="col">Units like it</th><th scope="col">Share in the master deed</th><th scope="col">Each unit's bill</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">One-bedroom</th><td data-label="Units like it">60 units</td><td data-label="Share in the master deed">0.65 percent each</td><td data-label="Each unit's bill">$5,850</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Two-bedroom</th><td data-label="Units like it">40 units</td><td data-label="Share in the master deed">0.95 percent each</td><td data-label="Each unit's bill">$8,550</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Three-bedroom</th><td data-label="Units like it">20 units</td><td data-label="Share in the master deed">1.15 percent each</td><td data-label="Each unit's bill">$10,350</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">All 120 units</th><td data-label="Units like it">120 units</td><td data-label="Share in the master deed">100 percent together</td><td data-label="Each unit's bill">$900,000 together</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The average is $7,500 a unit. Each unit's percentage is based on its value against the whole property when the condo was created. A unit with a higher percentage pays more of every assessment.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The percentages change only if every owner agrees. The exceptions are a phased condo, whose master deed gives the percentages for later phases, and a vote not to rebuild a unit.</p><div class="sa-calc"><p class="k">Work out your share</p><div style="display:grid;gap:.8rem"><label>What the repair costs<span class="u">$ <input id="saCost" type="number" min="0" step="10000" value="1200000" inputmode="decimal" oninput="c3Sa()" aria-label="What the repair costs"></span></label><label>What insurance and the reserve fund pay<span class="u">$ <input id="saSaved" type="number" min="0" step="10000" value="300000" inputmode="decimal" oninput="c3Sa()" aria-label="What insurance and the reserve fund pay"></span></label><label>Your unit's percentage in the master deed<span class="u"><input id="saPct" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="0.95" inputmode="decimal" oninput="c3Sa()" aria-label="Your unit's percentage in the master deed"> %</span></label></div><div class="out"><span>Your share</span><strong id="saShare">$8,550</strong></div><p class="note" id="saNote">The owners together owe $900,000.</p></div><script>function c3Sa(){var g=function(id){var e=document.getElementById(id);var v=e?parseFloat(e.value):NaN;return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0};var m=function(n){return "$"+Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US")};var s=document.getElementById("saShare"),t=document.getElementById("saNote");if(!s||!t)return;var rest=Math.max(0,g("saCost")-g("saSaved")),p=g("saPct");s.textContent=m(rest*p/100);t.textContent=rest>0?"The owners together owe "+m(rest)+".":"Insurance and the reserve fund pay all of it, so no special assessment is needed.";}document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",c3Sa);</script><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Your unit's percentage is in the master deed. We find it for any unit you are looking at.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="building-age"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Building age">How old are the condo buildings for sale on the Grand Strand?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most are more than 25 years old. Of 2,866 condos for sale in the local MLS, 65 percent are in buildings built before 2000. Only 4 percent are in buildings built in 2010 or later.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Condos for sale on the Grand Strand, by the year the building was built"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Condos for sale on the Grand Strand, by the year the building was built</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Year built, parts past their life</span><span role="columnheader">condos for sale</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built before 1980</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">past HUD's roof, elevator and window lives</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.335" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">328 (11%)</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 1980s</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">past HUD's roof, elevator and window lives</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">980 (34%)</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 1990s</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">past HUD's roof and elevator lives</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.568" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">557 (19%)</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 2000s</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">past HUD's flat roof life</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.898" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">880 (31%)</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 2010s</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.051" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">50 (2%)</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in 2020 or later</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.072" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">71 (2%)</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the 2,866 condos for sale in the local MLS, by the year built each listing gives. A building's parts may have been replaced since.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the city of Myrtle Beach, 72 percent of the condos for sale are in buildings built before 2000. In North Myrtle Beach the share is 46 percent.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What wears out, and when</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">HUD, the federal housing department, publishes how long building parts last when it plans repairs. A shingle roof gets 20 years, elevator machinery 20 and aluminum windows 35.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="How long building parts last in HUD's planning, beside two building ages"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">How long building parts last in HUD's planning, beside two building ages</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Building part</span><span role="columnheader">years</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Exterior paint</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.16" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">8 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Pool pumps and equipment</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">10 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Caulking and sealing</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">15 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Flat roof</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">15 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Shingle roof</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Elevator machinery</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Hydraulic elevator piston</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Asphalt parking lot</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">25 years</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A building from 1999, today</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.54" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">27 years old</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">EIFS (foam and stucco-look) walls</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">30 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Aluminum windows</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">35 years</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A building from 1989, today</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.74" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">37 years old</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Concrete or steel balconies</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.8" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">40 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Metal railings</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">50 years</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>HUD's estimated useful life table for family housing. The grey bars are buildings, not parts.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building built before 1990 has passed HUD's planning life for a flat or shingle roof, elevator machinery and aluminum windows at least once. That does not mean those parts were never replaced. We find out which ones were, from the reserve study and the minutes.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="votes-and-limits"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Votes and limits">Can a condo HOA charge a special assessment without a vote in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if the building's bylaws allow it. South Carolina's condo law leaves how owners are billed to each building's bylaws, which are recorded with the master deed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Where owners vote, a majority is at least 51 percent of the value, counted by each unit's percentage. In the example above, a three-bedroom with 1.15 percent has more say than a one-bedroom with 0.65 percent. Changing the system of running the building takes owners with two-thirds of the value.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is there a limit on a special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina's condo and HOA laws put no cap on a special assessment. They require no reserve study and no periodic structural inspection.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The one report they require is an engineer's or architect's condition report when apartments are converted to condos. The limits on a special assessment are in each building's master deed, covenants and bylaws.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A court will not second-guess a board's decision made within its authority, in good faith and without corrupt motives. That protection does not extend to a board that breaks its own master deed or bylaws. A South Carolina attorney can tell an owner whether a board did.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What happened when owners voted no</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At Shipyard Village, the owners voted the special assessment down. The board then put the repair costs into its operating budgets instead, about half in one year and the rest planned for the next. The court recorded that the board did not send that budget to owners for review, as its bylaws required.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How often owners here dispute them</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County had more HOA complaints than any other county in South Carolina: 140 of the 452 in the state's report for 2025. Complaints over fees or special assessments were an even larger share.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Horry County's share of South Carolina's HOA complaints in 2025"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Horry County's share of South Carolina's HOA complaints in 2025</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of complaint, Horry County's count</span><span role="columnheader">Horry County's share</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Complaints over fees or special assessments</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">30 of 76</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">All HOA complaints</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">140 of 452</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Complaints filed with the South Carolina Department of Consumer Affairs and kept in its report.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County complaints disputing fees or special assessments more than doubled from 2024 to 2025, from 12 to 30.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-we-spot-one"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How we spot one">How do we spot a special assessment before you make an offer?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the building's papers before you make an offer. They are the same papers Fannie Mae tells a lender to read. <a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The HOA documents a buyer receives</a> are only part of them.</p><ol style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Board minutes from the last 12 to 24 months.</strong> A board that is pricing a repair or hiring an engineer says so in its minutes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The association's list of special assessments and needed repairs.</strong> Fannie Mae tells lenders to ask for both.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Any engineer's or inspection report.</strong> If a structural or mechanical inspection was done in the last 3 years, the lender must get and read it too.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The reserve study, against the building's age.</strong> We compare what the fund holds with the parts past their HUD life. South Carolina does not require a reserve study, so some buildings have none. <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How much a building should have saved</a>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The budget and the book of receipts and expenses.</strong> A condo board must keep a detailed book of every receipt and expense, open to all owners.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The seller's disclosure.</strong> It asks: "Has the owners association levied any special assessments or similar charges?" It also asks about "any known common area problems".</li></ol><div class="sa-words"><p>Words in the minutes we look for</p><ul><li>special assessment</li><li>engineer's report</li><li>structural inspection</li><li>water intrusion</li><li>balconies</li><li>waterproofing</li><li>necessary repairs</li><li>added to the operating budget</li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In most resales the seller fills in that disclosure form before a contract. The answer can be yes, no or No Representation, which means the seller does not know. Foreclosure, estate and new-home sales, and sales where both sides agree in writing to skip it, are exempt.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Owners have the right to the records, so we get them through the seller. An owner may inspect and copy the last three years of owners' meeting minutes with five business days' written notice. For board minutes, the owner must also give a proper purpose and name the records.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="who-pays-at-sale"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who pays at sale">Who pays a special assessment when a condo sells?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller pays what was billed before closing, from the sale price. Unpaid assessments are paid after past-due property taxes and recorded mortgages.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the seller does not pay, the buyer is liable with the seller for everything owed up to closing, limited to the amount on the association's statement. A buyer who pays it can recover it from the seller.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Who pays a special assessment when a condo sells</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Where it stands at closing</th><th scope="col">Who pays</th><th scope="col">What we do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Billed before closing, not yet paid</th><td data-label="Who pays">The seller, from the sale price</td><td data-label="What we do">We get the association's statement</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Voted before closing, billed after</th><td data-label="Who pays">The buyer, unless the contract splits it</td><td data-label="What we do">We write the split into the offer</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Talked about, not yet voted</th><td data-label="Who pays">The buyer, once it is billed</td><td data-label="What we do">We read the minutes for it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We get the association's statement of what the seller owes before closing. <a href="/hoa/estoppel-and-transfer-fees/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The statement and its fee</a> have their own page. Selling a condo with an assessment pending? See <a href="/sell/sell-my-condo/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling a condo in Myrtle Beach</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Loans">Can a special assessment stop a condo loan?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Fannie Mae buys home loans from lenders. It will not buy a loan on any unit in a building whose special assessment pays for a critical repair that has not been fixed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For each special assessment, current or planned, the lender must learn its purpose, when it was approved, its original amount, what is left to collect and when it will be paid off.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What Fannie Mae counts as critical</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Repairs to items such as sea walls, elevators, waterproofing, stairwells, balconies, foundations, electrical systems and parking structures.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Mold, water intrusion and damaging leaks in the buildings.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Any unfunded repair over $10,000 per unit due within 12 months. A repair a special assessment already pays for is not counted.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A full or partial evacuation order for an unsafe condition. The building is ineligible until it is safe.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Damage limited to one or a few units does not count. Routine replacements paid from the budget, or by special assessments within Fannie Mae's rules, are not critical.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">FHA, VA and other loans have their own rules. <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Non-warrantable condos</a> explains buying in a building that does not meet Fannie Mae's.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="not-paying"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Not paying">Can I refuse to pay a special assessment, or pay it over time?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No owner can refuse a lawful share. Not using the common areas, or walking away from the unit, does not end it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No South Carolina statute gives an owner a right to a payment plan. Whether one is offered is the board's choice. An association that is a nonprofit corporation may borrow money and pledge its income, unless its articles say otherwise.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What happens if it goes unpaid</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An unpaid condo assessment is a lien on the unit, behind property taxes and recorded mortgages. The association can foreclose it in court like a mortgage, or sue for the money without foreclosing. During a foreclosure, a receiver can collect a reasonable rent from the owner.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A neighbor's unpaid share can become every owner's cost. When anyone buys a unit at the foreclosure sale of a recorded mortgage, the buyer does not owe the unit's unpaid assessments that came due between the recording of that mortgage and the day it took title. Those amounts become a common expense collected from all the owners.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In 2025, more than 500 associations in Horry County recorded a lien against at least one owner for unpaid charges. That is about the same as in 2024, and up from about 350 in 2021.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Associations that recorded a lien against at least one owner, Horry County"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Associations that recorded a lien against at least one owner, Horry County</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Year</span><span role="columnheader">associations</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2025</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.967" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 530</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2024</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.961" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 520</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2023</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.781" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 430</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2022</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.671" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 370</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2021</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.645" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 350</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2020</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.682" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 370</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2019</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.739" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 400</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2018</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.682" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 370</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2017</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.754" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 410</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2016</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.704" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 380</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2015</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.759" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 410</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2014</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.778" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 420</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2013</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.763" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 420</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2012</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.892" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 490</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2011</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.926" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 500</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2010</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 540</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the county's Register of Deeds index of condo and HOA liens, with spellings of one association's name merged, rounded to the nearest 10. A lien can be for any unpaid charge, not only a special assessment. A few liens filed under other document types are not counted.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA liens and foreclosure</a> has each step.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="tax-and-insurance"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Tax and insurance">Is a special assessment tax deductible, and does insurance pay it?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a home you live in, no. Association assessments are not deductible as real estate tax, because an association, not a government, charges them.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a condo you rent out, dues and assessments for maintenance of the common elements are deductible rental expenses. A special assessment for an improvement is not deductible, but your share can be recovered through depreciation.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Can you deduct a special assessment?</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">The condo</th><th scope="col">Assessment for maintenance</th><th scope="col">Assessment for an improvement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A home you live in</th><td data-label="Assessment for maintenance">Not deductible as real estate tax</td><td data-label="Assessment for an improvement">Not deductible as real estate tax</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A condo you rent out</th><td data-label="Assessment for maintenance">A deductible rental expense</td><td data-label="Assessment for an improvement">Recovered through depreciation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/hoa/tax-deductible/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Are HOA fees tax deductible</a> has the detail. The association must insure the building. What your own condo policy pays is on our <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">master insurance page</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="special-assessment-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Special assessment questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a condo special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A one-time bill from the condo association, on top of the regular dues, for a cost the budget and the reserve fund do not pay. Every owner pays a share, by the unit's percentage in the master deed.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much can a special assessment be?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At one Pawleys Island condo, the board proposed $88,398 per unit for extensive repairs and new windows and sliding doors. In our example, a 120-unit building that still needs $900,000 for a roof bills $5,850 to $10,350 a unit.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can an HOA charge a special assessment without a vote in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if the building's bylaws allow it. South Carolina's condo law leaves how owners are billed to each building's bylaws.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is there a limit on special assessments in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina's condo and HOA laws put no cap on a special assessment. The limits are in the building's master deed, covenants and bylaws.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who pays a special assessment at closing, the buyer or the seller?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller pays what was billed before closing, from the sale price. If it is not paid, the buyer is liable with the seller, up to the amount on the association's statement.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the seller have to disclose a special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In most resales, yes. The state's disclosure form asks whether the association has levied any special assessments, and the seller answers yes, no or No Representation.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I refuse to pay a special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. An unpaid assessment is a lien on the unit, and the association can foreclose it in court.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I pay a special assessment over time?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only if the board offers a payment plan. No South Carolina statute gives an owner a right to one.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a special assessment stop my loan?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Fannie Mae will not buy a loan on any unit in a building whose special assessment pays for a critical repair that has not been fixed.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is a special assessment tax deductible?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not on a home you live in. On a condo you rent out, an assessment for maintenance is a deductible rental expense, and one for an improvement is recovered through depreciation.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the difference between a special assessment and a reserve fund?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The reserve fund is money the association saves ahead for big repairs. A special assessment is a bill for what the reserve fund and the budget cannot pay.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-30</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 33-31</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/27603.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fisher v. Shipyard Village</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Updated_Property_Disclosure_Form.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure form</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/documents/eul_for_cna_e_tool.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD useful lives</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 527</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p530" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 530</a>, <a href="https://consumer.sc.gov/sites/consumer/files/Documents/HOA/2026_HOA_Complaint_Report.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDCA HOA report</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>. Condos for sale: our count of active listings by year built. Liens: our count of the county index. Not legal or tax advice; a South Carolina attorney can advise on your documents.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Have us check a building before you make an offer</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Send us the building's name. We request its minutes, its list of special assessments and its reserve study, if it has one, and tell you what we find.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us check a building</a></div></section></main>
